+    # =======================================================================
+    # Reading PDFs
+    #
+    # The parts of the PDF specification (ISO 32000-1) needed to find the text
+    # of a page, and where each word of it is:
+    #
+    # - the file structure: cross-reference tables and streams, object streams
+    #   and incremental updates, and rebuilding the cross-reference data by
+    #   scanning the file when it is damaged;
+    # - the stream filters FlateDecode (decompressed by inflate_data), LZWDecode,
+    #   ASCIIHexDecode, ASCII85Decode and RunLengthDecode;
+    # - fonts: simple fonts with WinAnsi, MacRoman or Standard encodings,
+    #   /Differences, the encodings built into Type 1 fonts, and the widths of
+    #   the standard Helvetica, Times and Courier fonts; composite (Type0) fonts
+    #   with Identity or embedded CMaps, including vertical ones; ToUnicode
+    #   CMaps and glyph widths;
+    # - the text operators of content streams, including text inside form
+    #   XObjects, on pages that are rotated or whose /MediaBox does not start
+    #   at 0, 0.
+    #
+    # It does not render anything, decrypt encrypted PDFs, or read text that
+    # exists only in images (scans). Damaged parts of a file are skipped with
+    # a warning.
+    #
+    # PDF objects are read as: null is `nothing`, booleans are Bool, numbers
+    # are Int or Float64, names are Symbols, strings are Vector{UInt8} (their
+    # bytes; what they mean depends on the font), arrays are Vector{Any},
+    # dictionaries are Dict{Symbol,Any}, a reference `num gen R` is the Pair
+    # num => gen, and a stream is a NamedTuple (dict, data) of its dictionary
+    # and its still-encoded bytes. Bare words, such as `obj` or the content
+    # stream operator `Tj`, are Strings.
+    # =======================================================================
+
+    is_ref(x) = x isa Pair{Int,Int}
+    is_stream(x) = x isa NamedTuple{(:dict, :data)}
